WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.936
[MUSICAL TONE]

00:00:07.752 --> 00:00:08.460
All right, y'all.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.040
It is an absolute
trip to be here

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.280
at RStudio Conference
talking to all of you

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.670
about the R for Data Science
Online Learning Community,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.830
sharing a little bit
about my personal story,

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.570
and then talking about
diversity, equity,

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.720
and inclusion in data science.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.780
And that's a lot to cover
in the next 15-20 minutes,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.130
but we can do it.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.320
The reason this is
so wild to be here

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.470
is that this is not something
that kids like me traditionally

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.780
grow up and do.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.570
I grew up to a poor,
working-class family

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.730
outside of Buffalo, New York.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.070
And I am the first
and only person

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.030
in my family to both go to
and graduate from college.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.310
And I really liked college
so much that I ended up

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:54.150
going to graduate school twice.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.660
And I did college at the--

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:56.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.270
Yeah.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.050
People feel me.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.190
I can tell already.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.043
I did college at the
"education at any cost" model.

00:01:03.043 --> 00:01:05.459
To give you some perspective,
I graduated from high school

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:08.040
in 1999, but I took
eight years to finish

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.080
my undergrad, which
means I graduated

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.600
into the recession of 2008.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.660
So there were lots of jobs
for people with degrees.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.030
So I have a ton
of student loans.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.980
I went through a
variety of careers.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.510
I did academia for
a little while.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.980
And I ultimately ended up
teaching high school science,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.150
and I loved it.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.190
But I'm not sure
if you know this.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.580
Teaching doesn't pay the bills.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.900
It is an amazing career.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.370
I recommend it for everybody.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.160
But if you are carrying six
figures worth of student loan

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.770
debt, teaching is
probably not going

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.150
to help you make ends meet.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.170
So I was in this
position in about 2014

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.910
where I was evaluating my life.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.320
And I knew a couple of things.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.290
One-- I really
liked video games.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.280
And to be very specific, I
really liked World of Warcraft.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.580
And to be even more specific,
in the spirit of vulnerability,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.750
I could play World of Warcraft
for like 12 to 15 hours a day.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.600
So there were some things
going on in my life.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.449
But I really liked video games.

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:09.650
[LAUGHING]

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.520
Really liked video
games, and data science

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.220
was becoming a thing.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.100
And it didn't seem like a fad,
but data science was like, OK,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.530
I have some background
that could maybe

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.720
put me in a position where
I could do data science.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.500
And guess what?

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.290
I don't think I need to go
back to school to become a data

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:26.620
scientist.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.620
So I think that maybe
I could do this thing.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.990
I could be a data
scientist and I could

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.060
change my financial future.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:38.790
So through a variety of weird
coincidences and situations,

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.180
I found myself interviewing
for this nebulously defined

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.630
data-ish position with an
eSports startup based out

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.440
of Dallas, Texas
called PvP Live.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.500
To this day, I don't know where
I got the courage to tell them

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.880
I was a data scientist.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:56.970
I don't.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.210
I was registered in the
Johns Hopkins Data Science

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.870
specialization from Coursera,
but I took none of the classes.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.850
Like most of you here, I'm sure.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.840
And I had taken a graduate
level statistics course

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.280
for one semester, and I knew a
little bit about this language

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.390
called R.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.370
But the fact remains
that I knew more

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.750
about data science than
anybody at the company,

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.500
so nobody knew that maybe
I wasn't a data scientist.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.110
So I said, I am
a data scientist.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.000
And they hired me.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.324
So I worked remotely
for a while,

00:03:29.324 --> 00:03:31.740
and then they told me to move
to Dallas or find a new job,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.357
so I moved to Dallas.

00:03:33.357 --> 00:03:35.190
And my first day on the
job, they were like,

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.060
congratulations.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.290
You're a manager.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.490
And I was like, cool.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.630
Maybe I don't even need
to learn data science.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:41.130
You know?

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.950
I'll just manage
data scientists.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:44.280
So that was really cool.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.840
And then six months
on the job, I'm

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.500
spending more and more of my
time managing data scientists

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.690
and helping them
learn data science.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.430
And I'm learning a
little bit as I go,

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.980
but really I'm learning a
lot about managing and being

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.184
at a startup.

00:03:57.184 --> 00:03:59.100
And this is six months
in the job and it's new

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.990
and it's exciting,
and this is amazing.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.300
I told this company I
was a data scientist,

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.040
and they hired me
as a data scientist.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.500
And then they promoted me as
a manager of data scientists.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.690
And I was like, this is so easy.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.730
You just tell people
you're a data scientist

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.780
and they hire you.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.320
So 12 months into the
job, without warning

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.410
and without notice, they
let us go without pay.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.600
And that is something
of a misnomer,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.860
because they told me
I could have equity.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.800
But if your company
makes zero revenue,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.720
your equity is still zero.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.850
I knew enough math to
know that this was not

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.010
going to work out in my favor.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.870
So I'm in Dallas.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.190
I've renewed my lease.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.500
I've gambled everything
on data science

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.740
and this eSports startup,
and I've kind of lost.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:42.160
So what do I do?

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.504
Do I go back to teaching?

00:04:43.504 --> 00:04:45.420
I know teaching is not
going to pay the bills,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.320
but I know I love it.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.180
But then I've got to
get certified in Texas,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.980
and that's a process.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.400
Or do I try to find something
data-related or data adjacent?

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.410
And I decided to see what
I could do with data.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.200
And maybe let go of the
data scientist title.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.330
Does it matter if I'm
called a data scientist,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.700
or does it matter if
I'm working with data?

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:03.750
And I was like, well,
I guess it matters

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.875
that I'm working with data.

00:05:04.875 --> 00:05:07.050
But this time, I'm going
to work for a company

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:08.400
that I believe in.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.540
I did believe in the
eSports startup for a while.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.630
But I was like, I need someone
with a little more clout.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.900
So I ended up working for the
Girl Scouts of Northeast Texas.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.600
And ultimately, at Teaching
Trust, which is where I am now.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.170
And I do strongly recommend
working for nonprofits

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:27.310
to start getting your feet
wet with data-related careers.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:28.685
I've had all sorts of titles.

00:05:28.685 --> 00:05:29.560
I've been an analyst.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.101
I've been in research
and evaluation.

00:05:31.101 --> 00:05:32.140
I've been in outcomes.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:34.930
And as long as you let go of
that data scientist title,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.780
I think that you can get
started with data science

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.800
just about anywhere.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.030
So I was in this position
where I'm working in nonprofits

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.940
and I'm doing analytical work,
but I'm moving into management.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.590
It turns out once
you're a manager,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:49.780
it's hard to not be
a manager ever again.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.460
Because people are like oh, no.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.960
We just want you to manage.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.314
So I'm here, and I'm
leveling up on the side.

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:57.730
And I'm like, I
want to get better

00:05:57.730 --> 00:05:59.246
at R. I kind of
know this language.

00:05:59.246 --> 00:06:00.370
I think it's really useful.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.220
I think it can make a lot
of what I do at work better.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.470
So as an educator,
I was like, I'm

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.470
going to teach myself R. I'm
going to get better at this,

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.600
but I'm going to be
transparent about my process,

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.210
and I'm going to share that
process with other people.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.787
So I took to social
media and Twitter,

00:06:15.787 --> 00:06:18.370
and I was tweeting about things,
and I was writing blog posts.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:19.960
And my first blog
posts were helping

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.440
people navigate that gap between
using your computer for Netflix

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.590
and using your computer
to do data science.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.510
And forget the gap
between Excel and R.

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.730
The gap between using
your computer for Netflix

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:34.720
and the gap between navigating
RStudio is very, very real.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.660
And people need those resources.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.714
So as I was doing this,
two things happened.

00:06:40.714 --> 00:06:42.130
Someone on Twitter
was like, there

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.350
is this really good book
called R for Data Science,

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:44.980
and it's free.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.210
And I was like, oh, cool.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.250
I will read the book.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.410
But then Hadley retweeted me.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.930
And this is the most popular
I've ever been on Twitter.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.450
It's probably the most popular
I will ever be on Twitter,

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:56.692
and that's OK.

00:06:56.692 --> 00:06:58.150
But I was benefiting
from something

00:06:58.150 --> 00:06:59.920
that, for some
people, we informally

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.740
refer to as the
"Hadley bump," right?

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.564
For 15 minutes, this is my
15 minutes of fame, right?

00:07:05.564 --> 00:07:07.480
And I'm like, I can take
this and run with it,

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:08.830
or I can forget about it.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.360
People are interested
in what I have

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.580
to say for a limited
amount of time,

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.190
so I have the opportunity to
take this book that I'm going

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.980
to read anyway, and why
not invite other people

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.510
to read it with me?

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.660
So that was how the R for
Data Science Online Learning

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.030
Community-- the concept started.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:26.710
But when Hadley
retweets you, you

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.420
have a limited
amount of time to get

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.621
people still interested
in what you're saying.

00:07:30.621 --> 00:07:32.870
I can't wait two weeks and
come back and be like, hey,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:33.910
I have this cool idea.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.600
You have to move.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.310
So it's all about finding
your opportunities, people.

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:40.900
So I have to come
up with this plan.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:42.610
And my idea is like,
well, I don't want

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:43.900
to spend any money on this.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.520
And I don't want
anybody else to have

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:46.962
to spend any money on this.

00:07:46.962 --> 00:07:48.670
We shouldn't have to
spend money to learn

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.230
how to do data science.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:53.480
We're repurposing things
we already know how to do.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.060
So I was like, it's
going to be free.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:57.476
It's going to be open to whoever
wants to be a part of it.

00:07:57.476 --> 00:08:00.670
And so the original idea was,
let's create a Slack group

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.860
and let's follow this
curriculum map that

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:06.070
goes through maybe two chapters
of the textbook every week.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:07.990
And we'll finish in
three to four months,

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:09.880
and we'll all be data
scientists at the end.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:10.520
We'll be pros.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:11.620
We'll be total experts.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.329
It's going to be amazing.

00:08:13.329 --> 00:08:15.370
And one thing we did with
this group that I think

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.470
is different from some
other online learning

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.500
communities is that
we made it very clear

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.430
that you had to
be nice to people.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.430
And you would be kicked
out if you weren't nice.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.220
This was going to be the kind
of place where you could come

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:31.469
and you could say, I only
have two panes in RStudio,

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:33.010
and I know I'm
supposed to have four,

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:34.990
and I don't know what to do.

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:37.030
And instead of someone
being like, loser.

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:38.330
You're so new at this.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.240
People would be like,
hey, you know what?

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.510
I can help you with that.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:44.432
And I'm going to stick with
you until we figure that out.

00:08:44.432 --> 00:08:46.390
And so that was the idea
for the kind of people

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:47.920
you were going to encounter.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.810
And then lastly,
we had this idea

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.880
of there being
mentors and learners.

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:53.410
So you self-identified.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.380
Am I here to mentor other people
in their learning journey,

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:57.790
or am I here to learn?

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.520
And maybe you were a learner
for the first three chapters,

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.590
and then you became a
mentor for other things.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:04.510
So it was this very
fluid definition.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.000
We weren't going to say,
oh, we've done this survey

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.190
and you are a mentor
and you are a learner.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:10.450
You got to identify.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:15.120
You got to take responsibility
for your role in the community.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:16.530
So expectations versus reality.

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:20.550
I could on about this--

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:22.466
I've got like three hours
of material on this.

00:09:22.466 --> 00:09:23.370
I will keep it brief.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.400
Essentially I was
like, no one's going

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:25.524
to want to do this with me.

00:09:25.524 --> 00:09:28.140
This is an awful
idea, but why not try?

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.610
I figured at the
end of two weeks,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.740
I'd be begging my friends
to do this with me.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:34.740
There'd be 12 of us,
maybe 25, maybe 50

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.190
in this online
learning community.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:36.990
And I'm like, that's fine.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:37.650
That's fine.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.210
It's fine.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:41.400
At the end of two weeks
when the invites went out,

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.760
we had over 600 members.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.670
Which for me meant that I'm
not there as a learner anymore.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.280
I'm there as a
community manager.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.550
And that was a surprise.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:54.450
That was something
I wasn't expecting.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.910
But as we moved
into things and I

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.000
started thinking about what
worked and what didn't work,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.290
there was a lot.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.480
But I would say
of the 600 people,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.020
within the first two weeks,
we lost half of our members.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:10.390
And we lost our members
for one of two reasons.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:12.851
One-- Slack was overwhelming.

00:10:12.851 --> 00:10:13.350
Right?

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:14.980
Most of us probably use
Slack and we're like,

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:16.380
oh, I'm so tired of this tool.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.360
It's amazing, but
stop sending me stuff.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:19.920
But you know how to navigate it.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.320
Not everybody knows
how to navigate Slack.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.720
Not everybody knows how to
turn their notifications off.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.720
And when you log in and all of
a sudden there's 450 messages,

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:28.620
you're done.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:29.830
You are out.

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:30.360
Right?

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.990
But we also had people who
couldn't install or navigate

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:35.460
R and RStudio.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:37.676
And that's where we
lost 50% of our members,

00:10:37.676 --> 00:10:40.050
was because we couldn't help
them get over those hurdles,

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.120
and we didn't even think
of those as necessarily

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.770
being the first hurdles.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.780
So again, this gap
from your computer

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.760
for Netflix to your
computer for data science

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.740
is very, very real.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.690
There was also a
misalignment, and probably

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:54.317
some miscommunication on my
part, about what we were doing.

00:10:54.317 --> 00:10:56.400
So some of the biggest
things was people showed up

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:59.100
and they were like, what
time do classes start?

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.579
I was like, they don't.

00:11:00.579 --> 00:11:02.370
We're just going to
read the book together,

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.720
and you're on your
own, and I'm not

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.053
going to tell you when to do
things or how to do things.

00:11:06.053 --> 00:11:07.500
So that was a big thing.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.210
We had people who
were like, what book?

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:10.830
What is the book?

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.380
So that was a little
bit lost in translation.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.720
And then another thing
was that not everybody

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.490
was tidyverse aligned.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:18.149
Now listen.

00:11:18.149 --> 00:11:20.190
If you're having trouble
installing R and RStudio

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:21.450
on your computer,
I don't expect you

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.783
to know the difference between
base R and the tidyverse.

00:11:23.783 --> 00:11:25.860
But if you're not
there to help people

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:28.410
learn the tidyverse, which
is a central organizing

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.320
principle of the
book, it's going

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.070
to be some rough sledding.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:33.540
So there was a lot to work out.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.004
And we also-- I didn't provide
any training for mentors.

00:11:36.004 --> 00:11:37.920
I was like, yeah, you
know how to be a mentor.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.080
Forget the fact that we
send teachers to school

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:41.281
to be certified in teaching.

00:11:41.281 --> 00:11:42.530
I was like, pssh, you're fine.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:43.290
Just be nice.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:43.950
It'll be good.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.480
It'll all work out.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:47.950
So at the end of round one.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:49.200
I was exhausted.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.535
This was-- I just
want to say, people

00:11:51.535 --> 00:11:53.920
are like, data
science is so hard.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.260
If you want a hard job,
go be a community manager,

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.197
because that is exhausting.

00:11:59.197 --> 00:12:01.780
If you know a community manager
in your life, give them a hug.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:02.946
Thank them for what they do.

00:12:02.946 --> 00:12:06.950
That is a thankless career,
and it is incredibly difficult.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.190
So this happened, right?

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:10.390
And I was exhausted.

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:11.576
I didn't read the book.

00:12:11.576 --> 00:12:12.700
I hadn't finished the book.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:15.220
I was spending all of my
time answering Twitter DMs,

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.380
creating content,
answering Slack messages,

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:18.880
helping connect people--

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.400
so I had really moved into
a community manager role,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.980
and it wasn't quite
what I expected.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:27.670
I will say that we did have one
person who copped to finishing

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:29.500
the entire curriculum.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.764
The guys at Hopkins
tell me that is well

00:12:31.764 --> 00:12:34.180
within the margin of error and
that I should be very, very

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:35.500
proud of that one person.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:36.370
And I am.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:38.800
I am also proud of myself
for doing something

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.060
before I was ready.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.730
And I'm really, really proud of
the people who were like, yeah,

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.189
this is a crazy idea,
but I'm going to do it.

00:12:45.189 --> 00:12:47.230
Everybody who came along
on that first adventure,

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.900
I am incredibly proud
of for taking a risk

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:51.760
and trying something new.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:55.330
So then people were like,
hey, when's round two?

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:59.140
So in round two, I was like,
I can't do this by myself.

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:00.310
Something needs to change.

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:03.970
And I said, what do you all
want to see in this community?

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.170
And people came back
with all sorts of ideas.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:08.110
And when they said, I
really want to see this.

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.630
I said, that is
a fantastic idea.

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:14.020
How can I support you in
making that a reality?

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:16.690
And what that did is it resulted
in people being like, oh, no.

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:18.325
I don't want to do the thing.

00:13:18.325 --> 00:13:19.207
And OK, that's great.

00:13:19.207 --> 00:13:20.540
Then it's not going to get done.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.410
But it also resulted
in people saying, well,

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.480
I need you to help me
to research software.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:26.950
Or I need you to
retweet something.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:30.370
Or I need you to just help me
talk in the group and recruit

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:31.120
people for this.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.610
And those were things
that I could do.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:36.580
So in round two, we had a
lot of amazing things happen.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.511
We had commitments
to office hours

00:13:38.511 --> 00:13:40.510
where people would give
up an hour of their week

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:42.190
and just show up and
answer questions.

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:42.940
We did GitHub.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.220
We did the chapters
out of the book.

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:49.170
We had YouTube tutorial videos
that community members made.

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:50.920
And then three things
that you're probably

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.650
most familiar with
is we have a website,

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:56.560
we have a Twitter account,
and we have Tidy Tuesday.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.600
These are not
things that I built.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:00.521
I made sure everybody
got in Slack

00:14:00.521 --> 00:14:02.770
and that everybody was happy
and well-behaved and nice

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:03.410
to each other.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.830
These are things that
community members have built,

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:09.040
and the community members
to this day maintained.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.190
If you want to get involved in
this online learning community,

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.100
you can go to that
website and sign up,

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:18.150
and I strongly
encourage you to do so.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:19.900
So when we were getting
through round two,

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:23.450
I'm realizing that the group
isn't what I set out to do,

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:23.950
right?

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.370
We're not going through a
text together and necessarily

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:29.920
learning at a specific pace.

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:31.840
But it's also an
amazing community,

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.030
and people are doing
amazing things.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:37.210
They're just not quite the
things that I want to do.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:41.320
So I am very excited to say
that the group was handed off

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:43.900
to a new set of leaders
who are enthusiastic

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:46.060
and incredibly talented
at what they do

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:47.800
in keeping this group running.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:49.410
And they are just--

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:52.480
I think they were saying that
there were over 2,500 members

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:53.500
today.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.150
So the community
continues to grow.

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:56.304
It continues to be active.

00:14:56.304 --> 00:14:57.970
It continues to be a
place where you can

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:01.780
go and give and get help in R.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.340
So in my time away
from the group,

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:06.680
I started to think
about a lot of things.

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:08.980
And I thought a
lot about my life

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:11.920
and how data science
has changed my life.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.830
Just doing this community,
just learning data science,

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:19.240
just being a data scientist
has changed my life.

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:22.090
It's changed the people
that I have access to,

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:24.940
it has given me opportunities
that I would never before

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:27.640
have had in my life, and
it has completely changed

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.740
my socioeconomic status.

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.830
Instead of living in a financial
crisis every single day

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.230
of my life, I'm
like two emergencies

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:37.300
away from a financial crisis.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:37.900
Right?

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.289
And that's a big deal when
you grow up in poverty

00:15:40.289 --> 00:15:41.830
and you can't put
food on the table--

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:43.120
to have that security.

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.810
So I was like, if data
science has done this for me,

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:48.630
then it can do it
for other people.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:50.440
And it's my
responsibility to help

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:54.610
make it easier for other
people move into data science.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:58.570
And I think we can all agree
that data and data science

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.570
is used every single
day about, by,

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:04.270
and for every single
one of us to make

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:06.830
choices and decisions for us.

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:08.830
Data is integral to
our lives, and it will

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:12.050
be for the foreseeable future.

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:15.220
So as I started to look around
data science as a whole,

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:16.720
and I was like,
data science affects

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:19.720
each and every one of us,
so each and every one of us

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:21.250
deserves a seat at the table.

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:26.460
So what I'm asking
people to do--

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:28.350
there is a laundry list
of a million things

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:31.080
I could ask you to do to start
getting more people into data

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:34.920
science, and making it easier to
bring people into data science,

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:36.810
and removing some
of the barriers.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.210
But I'm only going to
ask you to do one thing.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:41.160
I'm going to ask you
to be vulnerable.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.700
I'm going to ask you to help
me demystify data science.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.570
Share publicly on Twitter,
on your blog posts, in person

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:50.670
the things that you don't know.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:52.680
Share the things
that you're learning,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.160
and share the mistakes
that you've made.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.050
Data science isn't special.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:02.550
Data science isn't even
necessarily a difficult career.

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:04.710
I think the more--

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.480
there's no checklist
for data science

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:08.040
where we say, oh, great.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.050
I have done every
item on this list,

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.150
and then Hadley Wickham
descends from the heavens

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.069
and knights you
a data scientist,

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:16.920
and everything-- now you are
free to practice data science.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:20.280
Go do data science,
and share with people.

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:22.140
Because what data
science is is it's

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:25.740
about constantly learning
and growing and taking risks.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:28.860
I would argue that that is one
of the defining characteristics

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:32.460
of being in data science, that
you're constantly learning,

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:34.380
you're constantly
making mistakes,

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.690
and you're constantly
learning from mistakes.

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:38.580
And the more that
we normalize that

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:40.950
and we say, hey, this is
what us, the people who get

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.470
paid to be data scientists, do--

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.270
I think the more
that we can start

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:47.970
bringing in diverse
voices and making data

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:52.820
science as a community more
equitable and inclusive.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:56.359
With that, I think I might
be able to take one question?

00:17:56.359 --> 00:17:56.900
I don't know.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:58.725
Nina's my timekeeper.

00:17:58.725 --> 00:17:59.225
OK.

00:17:59.225 --> 00:17:59.725
[LAUGHING]

00:17:59.725 --> 00:18:00.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:01.630
Thank you.

00:18:11.641 --> 00:18:13.890
Or you don't have any
questions, and that's fine, too.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:15.780
You can always reach
out to me on Twitter.

00:18:19.259 --> 00:18:19.760
Hi.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:20.490
Thanks for your talk.

00:18:20.642 --> 00:18:22.510
And thanks for being
brave and vulnerable

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.040
and all those things.

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:28.060
So when you stepped
away from the community,

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:29.770
who was it that took
over, and what was

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.540
a part of that decision to--

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:33.520
Yeah, so two of the
people are in this room.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:36.534
One is a gentleman
named Dennis, who is--

00:18:36.534 --> 00:18:37.450
I can't remember the--

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:39.040
I think he's in Kenya.

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:40.930
And then we have Thomas
Mock in the back.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:42.910
He has got Tidy
Tuesday stickers.

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:44.770
I'm just putting that out there.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:46.795
And then Jon Harmon also
over here on the end.

00:18:46.795 --> 00:18:48.670
So these are the gentlemen
that are currently

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.500
running the community
and keeping it alive

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:53.410
and making sure everything
is going as planned.

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:59.460
[MUSICAL TONE]

